**What it does.** `buildURL(url, params, options)` (`:33-69`) turns `params` into a query string and appends it to `url` with `?` or `&`. Three paths, in priority order: a caller-supplied `serialize` function wins (`:51-52`); a `URLSearchParams` instance goes through its own `toString()` (`:54-55`); anything else goes through the internal params helper with an encoder that defaults to the local `encode` (`:56`). `encode` (`:14-22`) is `encodeURIComponent` plus six un-escapes: `%3A`→`:`, `%24`→`$`, `%2C`→`,`, `%20`→`+`, `%5B`→`[`, `%5D`→`]`. Falsy `params` returns `url` untouched (`:35-37`), and so does an empty serialization (`:59`).

## Hash fragments

**1. The fragment is deleted, not moved** (`:59-64`). `url.slice(0, hashmarkIndex)` keeps everything before `#` and drops the rest. It is also conditional on `serializedParams` being truthy, so `buildURL('/p#f', {})` returns `/p#f` while `buildURL('/p#f', {a:1})` returns `/p?a=1`. The server never sees a fragment either way, so this is string fidelity rather than request shape, but anything that reuses the returned string (a redirect target, a client-side route) loses the anchor.

Test: `buildURL('https://api.test/p#section-2', {a: 1})` should give `https://api.test/p?a=1#section-2`. It gives `https://api.test/p?a=1`.

## Encoding

**2. `[` and `]` go out unencoded** (`:20-21`). Both are gen-delims and are not legal in a query under RFC 3986 (`query = *( pchar / "/" / "?" )`, and `pchar` excludes them). Servers that parse bracket syntax (PHP, Rack, Rails) read them as array structure, so a value can change the shape of the request.

Test: `buildURL('/x', {q: 'a[b]'})` should give `/x?q=a%5Bb%5D`. It gives `/x?q=a[b]`.

**3. Space becomes `+`** (`:19`). Correct for `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` decoders, lossy for anything that decodes the query per RFC 3986, where `+` is a literal plus. A literal `+` in the input survives as `%2B`, so the two collapse only on the receiving side.

Test: send `{q: 'a b'}` and `{q: 'a+b'}` to a handler that decodes with `decodeURIComponent`. Both arrive as `a+b`.

**4. A lone surrogate throws a bare `URIError`** (`:15`). `encodeURIComponent` raises on an unpaired surrogate, and `JSON.parse('"\\ud800"')` produces exactly that, so JSON from a server or a user can make the request builder throw before anything is sent.

Test: `buildURL('/x', {q: JSON.parse('"\\ud800"')})` should encode to `%EF%BF%BD` or throw a named error. It throws `URIError: URI malformed` from inside `encode`.

## Custom serializers and options

**5. Serializer output is concatenated with no shape check** (`:52`, `:65`). A serializer returning a leading `?` or `&` produces a doubled delimiter. A non-string return is coerced by `+=`, so an object becomes `[object Object]`. A `''` return silently drops every param.

Test: `buildURL('/x', {a: 1}, {serialize: () => '?a=1'})` should give `/x?a=1`. It gives `/x??a=1`.

**6. A serializer can inject a fragment** (`:52`, `:65`). The hash strip at `:60-64` runs on `url` before the serialized string is appended, so a `#` in the serializer's output starts a fragment mid-query and everything after it never reaches the server. The default `encode` path is safe here because `#` becomes `%23`; only custom `serialize` or `encode` can do this.

Test: `buildURL('/x', {a: 1}, {serialize: () => 'a=1#frag'})` should reject or encode the `#`. It returns `/x?a=1#frag`.

**7. A custom `encode` is trusted verbatim** (`:39`, `:56`). This is the one with a security edge. The common "stop encoding my params" fix, `encode: v => v`, lets a value add parameters, so caller data decides the request shape.

Test: `buildURL('/x', {q: 'a&admin=true'}, {encode: v => v})` should keep one parameter. It produces `/x?q=a&admin=true`, which the server reads as two.

**8. `options.encode` is ignored for `URLSearchParams` input** (`:54-55`). The ternary takes `params.toString()` and never touches `_encode`, so the same options object behaves differently by input type. The two paths also disagree on brackets: the platform serializer percent-encodes them, so `{q: 'a[b]'}` yields `q=a[b]` while `new URLSearchParams({q: 'a[b]'})` yields `q=a%5Bb%5D`.

Test: `buildURL('/x', new URLSearchParams({q: 'a b'}), {encode: () => 'X'})` should invoke the encoder. It returns `/x?q=a+b` and the encoder is never called.

**9. `options.encode` is never checked to be a function** (`:39`). A truthy non-function goes straight into the params helper and fails there, far from the mistake. This one depends on the helper's contract, which I did not read.

Test: `buildURL('/x', {a: 1}, {encode: 'nope'})` should throw a message naming `encode`. It throws a `TypeError` from inside the params helper.

**10. `options.encode` is read before `options` is normalized** (`:39` before `:41-45`). When `options` is a function it is rewritten to `{serialize: options}` afterwards, so an `encode` property hung on that function would be picked up from the wrong object. Harmless in practice, wrong in principle.

Test: `const fn = () => 'a=1'; fn.encode = () => 'X'; buildURL('/x', {a: 1}, fn)` should ignore `fn.encode`. It uses it.

## URL assembly

**11. A trailing `?` or `&` leaves an empty parameter** (`:65`). The check is "does a `?` exist anywhere", not "does the query already end with a delimiter".

Test: `buildURL('/x?', {a: 1})` should give `/x?a=1`. It gives `/x?&a=1`.

## Checked and fine

The six `replace` calls at `:16-21` cannot double-decode: a literal `%3A` in a value encodes to `%253A`, which does not contain `%3A`, so no value can smuggle a decoded character through. `:`, `$` and `,` are legal in a query, so un-escaping those three is correct. CRLF and `#` inside a value are encoded at `:15` and never un-escaped, so no header or fragment injection through the default path. The fragment strip at `:60-64` runs before the `?` check at `:65`, so a `?` living inside a fragment does not produce a stray `&`.

One doc bug: the comment at `:7-8` says `encode` replaces those characters with their URI-encoded counterparts. It does the reverse.

